Sometimes. Empathy can become self-erasure—feeling everyone else so intensely that you abandon duty, judgment, or your own boundaries.

A man may need to understand another person’s pain and still say no, impose consequences, leave, fight through discomfort, or do what responsibility requires. That isn’t necessarily cruelty. It’s refusing to let empathy overrule conscience.

The stronger form is usually not becoming unfeeling. It’s retaining empathy without becoming governed by it. Mercy remains available—but it doesn’t hold the steering wheel.